"{\"url\":\"https://registry.isometric.com/project/prj_1K56VP7FE1S0QDSH\",\"title\":\"Biochar production using dead areca nut biomass — Isometric\",\"head\":[\"UNDER VALIDATION\",\"BICRS\",\"Biochar production using dead areca nut biomass\",\"Earthtree Enviro\",\"->\",\"PROJECT DETAILS\",\"Expected first certificates\",\"2026\",\"Project code\",\"DCCX\",\"Crediting period\",\"01 Jan 2026 to 31 Dec 2030\"],\"issued_header\":null,\"buffer_header\":null,\"crediting\":null,\"verifier\":null,\"code\":\"DCCX\",\"approval\":\"\",\"protocol_current\":\"Biochar Production and Storage v1.2\",\"protocol_baseline\":null,\"cert_issued\":null,\"cert_retired\":null,\"cert_buffer\":null,\"reversal\":null,\"showing\":[\"Showing 2 of 2\"],\"tables\":[{\"heads\":[\"Submission date\",\"Title\",\"Download\"],\"rows\":[[\"19 May 2026\",\"Public Comment Summary\",\"PDF | ->\"],[\"16 Apr 2026\",\"PDD\",\"PDF | ->\"]]}],\"links\":[{\"h\":\"https://earthtree.in\",\"t\":\"Earthtree Enviro\"},{\"h\":\"/protocol/biochar/1.2\",\"t\":\"Biochar Production and Storage v1.2\"},{\"h\":\"/supplier/spl_1K56VGNBH1S02CPX\",\"t\":\"Learn more\"},{\"h\":\"https://registry.isometric.com/standard#validation-and-verification-process\",\"t\":\"Isometric Standard\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1K56VGNBH1S02CPX/prj_1K56VP7FE1S0QDSH/pjd_1KRZWVXRT1S0FEDW/public_comment_summary.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1K56VGNBH1S02CPX/prj_1K56VP7FE1S0QDSH/pjd_1KPAWSKBM1S0P158/pdd.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"}],\"text\":\"UNDER VALIDATION\\nBICRS\\nBiochar production using dead areca nut biomass\\nEarthtree Enviro\\n->\\nPROJECT DETAILS\\nExpected first certificates\\n2026\\nProject code\\nDCCX\\nCrediting period\\n01 Jan 2026 to 31 Dec 2030\\nPROTOCOL DETAILS\\nCurrent protocol version\\nBiochar Production and Storage v1.2\\nSummary\\n\\nWe are working with farming communities in Garo Hills, Meghalaya (India) where approximately 500 hectares of Areca Nut plantations have been devastated by disease. The trees are no longer economically viable, and in the absence of intervention, farmers would either abandon the land or resort to open burning/decomposition, leading to uncontrolled carbon emissions and worsening financial stress.\\n\\nMeghalaya, India\\nHow carbon is being removed\\n\\nBiomass Carbon Removal and Storage (BiCRS) involves storing the carbon contained in waste biomass—organic material from living or recently living organisms. BiCRS works by halting or slowing the decomposition of biomass, enabling the carbon it contains to be stored long-term. This can be achieved by converting biomass into materials that decompose more slowly—such as biochar or bio-oil—or by storing it in environments where decomposition is slowed. For BiCRS to count as carbon removal, the biomass must be replenished to ensure additional carbon dioxide is drawn from the atmosphere.\\n\\nLearn more\\nProject timeline\\nCompleted 15 May 2026\\nPublic comment period\\nCompleted 2026\\nInitial documentation submission\\nCompleted 2026\\nAudit kick-off\\nCompleted 2026\\nSite visit\\nExpected 2026\\nIN PROGRESS\\nApproval date\\n\\nIsometric officially approves the project having reviewed the validation report produced by the VVB, documenting the activities, results, and conclusion of the audit. A full list of content requirements for the report can be found in the Isometric Standard.\\n\\nExpected 2026\\nInitial credit issuance\\nProject documentation\\nSubmission date\\tTitle\\tDownload\\n19 May 2026\\tPublic Comment Summary\\tPDF\\n->\\n\\n16 Apr 2026\\tPDD\\tPDF\\n->\\nShowing 2 of 2\\nPrevious\\nNext\"}"